## Troubleshooting

### "GLIBC_2.25 not found" or "GLIBC_2.28 not found" at startup

You have a pip-installed wheel of `lxml` (or similar) shadowing the
conda-forge build. Fix:

```bash
conda activate tofu
pip uninstall -y lxml flask flask-compress requests psutil trafilatura \
                 playwright pillow python-pptx mcp
conda install -c conda-forge -y --force-reinstall \
    flask 'flask-compress>=1.14' 'requests>=2.31' psutil \
    'trafilatura>=1.6' 'playwright>=1.40' pillow python-pptx 'lxml>=4.9' 'mcp>=1.0'
```

The one-command installer does this automatically; this is only needed
if you've manually run `pip install` against the env.

### Conda solver hangs forever

Almost always an outdated `conda`. Update first:

```bash
conda update -n base -c conda-forge -y conda
conda install -n base -c conda-forge -y conda-libmamba-solver
conda config --set solver libmamba
```

If that's not feasible (corp-managed conda), pass `--force-sibling-conda`
to the installer — it'll drop a private Miniforge next to the project
directory and use that, leaving your system conda untouched.

### PostgreSQL "data directory was created by major version X" error

Your `data/pgdata/` was initialized by a different PG major than the one
the installer just placed in your env. Two options:

1. **Keep your data, fall back to SQLite:** the installer does this
   automatically by default. Nothing to do.
2. **Re-initialize PG:** pass `--reinit-pgdata`. The installer renames
   the old `pgdata` to `pgdata.bak.<timestamp>` and creates a fresh one.

### Behind a corporate proxy that blocks GitHub releases

The Miniforge download will fail. Workarounds:

- Pre-download `Miniforge3-<platform>.sh` manually and set
  `TOFU_MINIFORGE_LOCAL=/path/to/Miniforge3-...sh` before running
  `install.sh`.
- Or set `TOFU_MINIFORGE_MIRRORS="https://your-mirror/..."` (one URL per
  line) — the installer tries each in order before giving up.

### Install log

Every `install.sh` run writes a full transcript (ANSI colours stripped)
to `<install-dir>/logs/install-YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.log`. Attach it when
filing an issue.
